            "declaration_text": "Run one pixel-art command on a drawing or image you already made. This is\nthe engine's own command line: it checks work, explains it and improves it.\n\nCommands: check and explain (read-only):\n- lint <id> [--rules outline_gap,orphan_pixel,…] — pixel-art defects, each\n  naming the node and command that drew them\n- inspect <id> --region x0,y0,x1,y1 --scale 10 — a zoomed crop with its\n  exact colours\n- measure <id> --at x,y — what is at a pixel and who drew it\n- explain <id> — resolved geometry, per-command footprints, before rendering\n- motion / storyboard / silhouette / reveal / clusters / parts <id> — reports\n- score <id> — a 0-100 pixel-art discipline score with a breakdown\n- compare <id> <id> — two results side by side\nImprove (returns a NEW drawing id):\n- shade <id> --band 2 --light=-1,-1 — banded shading along a colour ramp\n- selout <id> — selective outlining; reoutline / clean-lines / aa / mirror\n- cluster <id> --at x,y --action recolor --color <name> — one guarded cluster\n- repair <id> — deterministic cleanup of drift, strays, fringe and noise\nSheets and frames (returns a NEW image id):\n- split <image> --grid 3x3 — a sprite sheet, strip or grid as an animation,\n  one frame per cell (columns x rows)\n- join <image> <image> … [--as sheet|rows|animation] [--columns n] — pictures\n  or animations as one sprite sheet, one per row, or one animation\n- scale <image> --by 4 — the same picture or animation bigger, each pixel a\n  4x4 block (a sticker, a print); nothing is redrawn\nA result comes back as an image in the reply AND as a no-auth URL the reply\nprints, `/mcp/pixel-tools/<id>/image.png` (`frames.png` for the frames of a\nmulti-frame one) — so a sheet you joined is both readable inline and\ndownloadable. Sheets hold at most 1024 px a side; `join` refuses a larger\none rather than shrinking the art, and says how many frames do fit.\nCraft operations need colour ramps: run `extract-palette <id>` first, then\npass `--ramps skin=dark,mid,light`.\nAuthoring: `edit <id> --changes '[…]'` applies exact pixel operations and\n`draw <id> --recipe '{…}'` builds or revises a layered scene; both take JSON\nwhose fields are NOT listed here. Colours in that JSON are always `#RRGGBB`\nor `#RRGGBBAA` with alpha `00` or `ff` — `rgb()` and bare hex are refused,\nand nothing strips the `#`, since argv is a JSON list rather than a shell\nline. Every `edit` change carries `operation`, `layer`, `frames` (ONE-based)\nand `reason`; coordinates are zero-based and a `region` is `[x0,y0,x1,y1]`\ninclusive. Then its own fields: `patch_pixels` takes `pixels`, a list of\n`[x,y,expected,replacement]` (1-10000, each assigned only where the pixel\nalready holds `expected`, so a mismatch fails the pass); `copy_region` takes\n`source_layer`, `source_frame`, `region`, `at` `[x,y]`; `map_colors` takes\n`region` and `colors` `{\"#from\":\"#to\"}` (alpha must match); `flood_fill`\n`seed`/`expected`/`color`; `brush` `points` plus `color`; `outline` `mode`\n(outside|inside|recolor|erase). The full contracts and any command's flags\nare one free call away: `describe edits | paint | outline | draw | animation\n| motion | scenes | review`, `<command> -h`, `describe cli`.\nFree for subscribers.\n\nPixelLab AI-powered pixel art generation service.\n\nUse of this service is subject to PixelLab's Terms of Service:\nhttps://pixellab.ai/termsofservice\n\nThis MCP server provides programmatic access to generate:\n- Characters with multiple directional views\n- Character animations\n- Isometric tiles\n- Top-down tilesets for game maps\n- Sidescroller tilesets for platformer games\n\nAll generation operations are non-blocking and return job IDs for status tracking.\nRequires PixelLab subscription (with credit fallback support).\n\n**Pixel tools (free, exact, you are the artist)**: one command line, pixelart_workbench(argv=[...]), inspects, edits, draws and animates pixel art by your own decisions; combine it with the AI tools above. Start with pixelart_workbench(argv=['describe', 'start'], mode='low'|'high') — low acts directly with few tokens, high plans out loud and reviews every frame. Animated effects (spells, hits, explosions, auras): vfx_workbench(argv=['describe', 'start']) — same ids, exports to the user's My effects.\n\n**Git access**: Clone and push game project source code with standard git.\nUse `list_projects` to see your projects and their git clone URLs.\n\nexec tool declaration:\n```ts\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__pixellab__pixelart_workbench(args: {\n  // One command line, e.g. 'lint <drawing_id> --rules outline_gap' or ['edit', '<id>', '--changes', '[…]']. Omit it for the playbook. A subject is an id or an image reference — never a file path; the server writes the output and returns a new id. A reference is `character:<id>:<direction>` for a rotation, `character:<id>:<direction>:<animation>` for every frame of that animation (the display name, type or [group: ...] id get_character prints), the same two forms for `object:<id>` (a one-direction object's direction is the literal `unknown`), `gallery:<id>`, `job:<id>`, `upload:<id>`, a data URL, an https link or base64. That is how a stored animation's pixels come back inline without downloading anything. Start with 'describe cli' for the full command list.\n  argv?: string | Array<string> | null;\n  // low (default): act directly, fewest tokens — one decisive image per result. high: think openly — fuller evidence and a review checklist; write your plan and observations in `notes`. Use the mode the user asked for.\n  mode?: \"low\" | \"high\";\n  // What you observed and what this step should achieve, in a few sentences (high mode: always; low: optional). On the first call, include the user's request. Stored with the step to improve these tools.\n  notes?: string | null;\n}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\n```",
